  2380	/**Function*************************************************************
  2381	
  2382	  Synopsis    [Returns 1 if a fanout/load proxy should reject this cut.]
  2383	
  2384	  Description [Mode 1 is the classic map -f guard. Mode 2 is the stmap1
  2385	  selective guard, which only rejects wider cuts when the node has higher
  2386	  estimated fanout. Mode 3 is the stmap2 guard, which uses the same fanout
  2387	  thresholds only during area recovery and only when the current phase has
  2388	  slack over the mapper required time. Mode 4 is the stmap3 guard, which
  2389	  narrows the intervention to high-fanout nodes with only shallow positive
  2390	  slack. Mode 5 is the stmap4 split-phase guard: stmap1-style guarding in
  2391	  delay matching and stmap2-style slack-aware guarding in area recovery.
  2392	  Mode 6 is the stmap5 shape-gated split-phase guard: the delay pass only
  2393	  uses stmap1-style guarding in very large mapper graphs, while area recovery
  2394	  keeps the stmap2 slack-aware guard. Mode 7 is the stmap6 area-sensitive
  2395	  shape-gated guard: the delay pass keeps the stmap5 graph-size gate, small
  2396	  mapper graphs keep the stmap2 slack-aware recovery guard, and exact-area
  2397	  recovery on larger graphs checks the cut after matching so materially cheaper
  2398	  cuts can override the fanout/load proxy. Mode 8 is the stmap7 criticality-
  2399	  sensitive version of mode 7: exact-area recovery on larger graphs allows the
  2400	  override only with deeper mapper slack and outside the highest fanout bucket.
  2401	  Mode 9 is the stmap8 arrival-bounded version of mode 8: the area-saving
  2402	  override also requires the candidate match to stay within one quarter of an
  2403	  inverter delay of the existing mapper arrival. Mode 10 is the stmap9
  2404	  middle-slack area-relief version of mode 9: it keeps the highest fanout
  2405	  bucket protected, but in the one-to-two inverter slack bucket it allows a
  2406	  wide cut only when it is arrival-neutral and saves at least one inverter
  2407	  area. Mode 11 is the stmap10 depth-qualified version of mode 10: the
  2408	  middle-slack relief is allowed only for shallow and medium-depth nodes.
  2409	  Mode 12 is the stmap11 tight-depth version of mode 11: the middle-slack
  2410	  relief is allowed only for shallower nodes. Mode 13 is the stmap12
  2411	  fanout-bucket split version of mode 12: the tight-depth middle-slack relief
  2412	  is allowed only in the lower moderate fanout bucket. Mode 14 is stmap13,
  2413	  which keeps mode 13 behavior while recording guard-hit counters. Mode 15 is
  2414	  stmap14, which keeps the same bucket and counters but requires a stronger
  2415	  middle-slack area-flow saving. Mode 16 is stmap15, which restores the
  2416	  one-inverter relief threshold only after the current run has observed a high
  2417	  lower-moderate/middle-slack exact-area profile. Mode 17 is stmap16, which
  2418	  keeps mode 16 behavior and prints per-relief diagnostics. Mode 18 is stmap17,
  2419	  which uses the one-inverter post-profile threshold only for candidates with
  2420	  materially better mapper arrival. Mode 19 is stmap18, which keeps mode 18
  2421	  decisions and adds near-miss diagnostics for candidates rejected by the
  2422	  timing-quality/profile fallback. Mode 20 is stmap19, which allows the
  2423	  one-inverter threshold before profile-open only for candidates with the same
  2424	  material mapper-arrival improvement. Mode 21 is stmap20, which keeps the
  2425	  mode 20 pre-profile seed but restricts it to shallow nodes. Mode 22 is
  2426	  stmap21, which keeps the shallow seed and admits deeper pre-profile seeds
  2427	  only when the arrival gain is unusually large. Mode 23 is stmap22, which
  2428	  relaxes the deep pre-profile seed boundary to a moderate arrival gain. Mode
  2429	  24 is stmap23, which keeps the strong deep seeds but allows moderate deep
  2430	  seeds only when the existing mapper slack is near the critical side of the
  2431	  middle-slack window. Mode 25 is stmap24, which tightens the moderate deep
  2432	  slack gate to the lower edge of that critical window. Mode 26 is stmap25,
  2433	  which keeps stmap24 and admits at most one moderate deep seed from the next
  2434	  slack band as an ablation diagnostic. Mode 27 is stmap26, which skips the
  2435	  first such moderate seed and admits only the second qualifying seed. Mode 28
  2436	  is stmap27, which reopens the next slack band except for the high-reference,
  2437	  low-slack, stronger-moderate signature isolated by stmap26. Mode 29 is
  2438	  stmap28, which replaces that hard signature blocker with a high-reference
  2439	  moderate-seed area penalty that scales with reference count, slack risk, and
  2440	  arrival-gain risk. Mode 30 is stmap29, which keeps the same seed band but
  2441	  makes the penalty a continuous load-risk gradient. Mode 31 is stmap30,
  2442	  which keeps that moderate-seed gradient and adds a smaller continuous
  2443	  penalty to high-reference strong deep seeds. Mode 32 is stmap31, which keeps
  2444	  mode 31's moderate penalty but dampens the strong-seed gain slope. Mode 33
  2445	  is stmap32, which adds a cut-leaf load proxy to the strong-seed penalty. Mode
  2446	  34 is stmap33, which normalizes that load proxy by supergate fanout limit.
  2447	  Mode 35 is stmap34, which uses a bounded sublinear load/drive curve. Mode
  2448	  36 is stmap35, which preserves that mapper curve while command-level SCL
  2449	  load/max-cap diagnostics are emitted. Mode 37 is stmap36, which strengthens
  2450	  the bounded curve according to direct SCL over-cap severity measured by a
  2450	  the bounded curve according to direct SCL over-cap severity measured by a
  2451	  command-layer first pass. Mode 38 is stmap37, which applies that feedback
  2452	  only when the current mapper node appears in the first-pass over-cap
  2453	  hotspot set. Mode 39 is stmap38, which broadens the local association to
  2454	  command-collected fanout users and candidate cut leaves. Mode 40 is stmap39,
  2455	  which replaces the static hotspot neighborhood with command-collected
  2456	  downstream consumer pressure. Mode 41 is stmap40, which preserves those
  2457	  pressure ratios in a dense AIG-ID lookup so medium-pressure reconstruction
  2458	  IDs are not discarded by a capped table. Mode 42 is stmap41, which uses the
  2459	  same dense transfer but weights pressure by first-pass SCL timing criticality
  2460	  before it can increase the strong-seed area margin. Mode 43 is stmap42, which
  2461	  propagates sink criticality from over-cap consumers into their fanin pressure
  2462	  cones with a bounded raw-pressure floor. Mode 44 is stmap43, which keeps the
  2463	  sink-pressure strong-seed protection but gates moderate deep area penalties
  2464	  by the same sink-pressure vector so unpressured paths can recover area. Mode
  2465	  45 is stmap44, which keeps the strong-seed sink-pressure rule but requires
  2466	  node and cut-leaf pressure agreement before applying the moderate penalty.
  2467	  Mode 46 is stmap45, which also requires that pressure agreement before
  2468	  moderate deep recovery can use the one-inverter admission margin. Mode 47
  2469	  is stmap46, which preserves the pressure-agreement rule for broad moderate
  2470	  candidates but admits high-arrival-gain tight-class seeds through a narrow
  2471	  exception. Mode 53 is stmap52, which returns to the stmap45
  2472	  pressure-agreement rule but caps the moderate pressure penalty for
  2473	  area-saving candidates that still have local pressure agreement. Mode 54 is
  2474	  stmap53, which keeps the stmap52 shape and adds a severe-feedback-only
  2475	  pressure-near moderate exception. Mode 55 is stmap54, which keeps the
  2476	  reviewed stmap53 branch and adds a cut-only one-sided pressure exception with
  2477	  stronger arrival-gain gating. Mode 56 is stmap55, which keeps that branch but
  2478	  caps cut-only exceptions to at most 1.25 inverter areas of local area-save.
  2479	  Mode 57 is stmap56, which relaxes only that cap to 1.35 inverter areas.]
  2480	
  2481	  SideEffects []
  2482	
  2483	  SeeAlso     []
  2484	
  2485	***********************************************************************/
  2486	static int Map_MatchSkipCutForFanout( Map_Man_t * p, Map_Node_t * pNode, Map_Cut_t * pCut, int fPhase )
  2487	{
  2488	    Map_Cut_t * pCutBest;
  2489	    Map_Match_t * pMatchBest;
  2490	    float Slack, SlackMargin, SlackLimit;
  2491	
  2492	    if ( !p->fSkipFanout )
  2493	        return 0;
  2494	    if ( p->fSkipFanout == 2 )
  2495	        return Map_MatchCutHasStmapFanoutRisk( pNode, pCut );
  2496	    if ( p->fSkipFanout == 3 )
  2497	    {
  2498	        if ( p->fMappingMode < 1 || p->fMappingMode > 3 )
  2499	            return 0;
  2500	        pCutBest = pNode->pCutBest[fPhase];
  2501	        if ( pCutBest == NULL )
  2502	            return 0;
  2503	        pMatchBest = pCutBest->M + fPhase;
  2504	        if ( pMatchBest->pSuperBest == NULL )
  2505	            return 0;
  2506	        Slack = pNode->tRequired[fPhase].Worst - pMatchBest->tArrive.Worst;
  2507	        SlackMargin = p->pSuperLib ? p->pSuperLib->tDelayInv.Worst : 0.0;
  2508	        if ( Slack <= SlackMargin + p->fEpsilon )
  2509	            return 0;
  2510	        return Map_MatchCutHasStmapFanoutRisk( pNode, pCut );
  2511	    }
  2512	    if ( p->fSkipFanout == 4 )
  2513	    {
  2514	        if ( p->fMappingMode < 1 || p->fMappingMode > 3 )
  2515	            return 0;
  2516	        pCutBest = pNode->pCutBest[fPhase];
  2517	        if ( pCutBest == NULL )
  2518	            return 0;
  2519	        pMatchBest = pCutBest->M + fPhase;
  2520	        if ( pMatchBest->pSuperBest == NULL || p->pSuperLib == NULL )
  2521	            return 0;
  2522	        Slack = pNode->tRequired[fPhase].Worst - pMatchBest->tArrive.Worst;
  2523	        SlackMargin = p->pSuperLib->tDelayInv.Worst;
  2524	        SlackLimit = 2.0 * SlackMargin;
  2525	        if ( Slack <= p->fEpsilon || Slack > SlackLimit + p->fEpsilon )
  2526	            return 0;
  2527	        return Map_MatchCutHasStmapFanoutRisk( pNode, pCut );
  2528	    }
  2529	    if ( p->fSkipFanout == 5 )
  2530	    {
  2531	        if ( p->fMappingMode == 0 )
  2532	            return Map_MatchCutHasStmapFanoutRisk( pNode, pCut );
  2533	        if ( p->fMappingMode < 1 || p->fMappingMode > 3 )
  2534	            return 0;
  2535	        pCutBest = pNode->pCutBest[fPhase];
  2536	        if ( pCutBest == NULL )
  2537	            return 0;
  2538	        pMatchBest = pCutBest->M + fPhase;
  2539	        if ( pMatchBest->pSuperBest == NULL )
  2540	            return 0;
  2541	        Slack = pNode->tRequired[fPhase].Worst - pMatchBest->tArrive.Worst;
  2542	        SlackMargin = p->pSuperLib ? p->pSuperLib->tDelayInv.Worst : 0.0;
  2543	        if ( Slack <= SlackMargin + p->fEpsilon )
  2544	            return 0;
  2545	        return Map_MatchCutHasStmapFanoutRisk( pNode, pCut );
  2546	    }
  2547	    if ( p->fSkipFanout == 6 )
  2548	    {
  2549	        if ( p->fMappingMode == 0 )
  2550	        {
  2551	            if ( p->vMapObjs->nSize <= 20000 )
  2552	                return 0;
  2553	            return Map_MatchCutHasStmapFanoutRisk( pNode, pCut );
  2554	        }
  2555	        if ( p->fMappingMode < 1 || p->fMappingMode > 3 )
  2556	            return 0;
  2557	        pCutBest = pNode->pCutBest[fPhase];
  2558	        if ( pCutBest == NULL )
  2559	            return 0;
  2560	        pMatchBest = pCutBest->M + fPhase;
  2561	        if ( pMatchBest->pSuperBest == NULL )
  2562	            return 0;
  2563	        Slack = pNode->tRequired[fPhase].Worst - pMatchBest->tArrive.Worst;
  2564	        SlackMargin = p->pSuperLib ? p->pSuperLib->tDelayInv.Worst : 0.0;
  2565	        if ( Slack <= SlackMargin + p->fEpsilon )
  2566	            return 0;
  2567	        return Map_MatchCutHasStmapFanoutRisk( pNode, pCut );
  2568	    }
  2569	    if ( p->fSkipFanout == 7 )
  2570	    {
  2571	        if ( p->fMappingMode == 0 )
  2572	        {
  2573	            if ( p->vMapObjs->nSize <= 20000 )
  2574	                return 0;
  2575	            return Map_MatchCutHasStmapFanoutRisk( pNode, pCut );
  2576	        }
  2577	        if ( p->fMappingMode >= 1 && p->fMappingMode <= 3 && p->vMapObjs->nSize <= 8000 )
  2578	        {
  2579	            pCutBest = pNode->pCutBest[fPhase];
  2580	            if ( pCutBest == NULL )
  2581	                return 0;
  2582	            pMatchBest = pCutBest->M + fPhase;
  2583	            if ( pMatchBest->pSuperBest == NULL )
  2584	                return 0;
  2585	            Slack = pNode->tRequired[fPhase].Worst - pMatchBest->tArrive.Worst;
  2586	            SlackMargin = p->pSuperLib ? p->pSuperLib->tDelayInv.Worst : 0.0;
  2587	            if ( Slack <= SlackMargin + p->fEpsilon )
  2588	                return 0;
  2589	            return Map_MatchCutHasStmapFanoutRisk( pNode, pCut );
  2590	        }
  2591	        if ( p->fMappingMode == 1 )
  2592	        {
  2593	            pCutBest = pNode->pCutBest[fPhase];
  2594	            if ( pCutBest == NULL )
  2595	                return 0;
  2596	            pMatchBest = pCutBest->M + fPhase;
  2597	            if ( pMatchBest->pSuperBest == NULL )
  2598	                return 0;
  2599	            Slack = pNode->tRequired[fPhase].Worst - pMatchBest->tArrive.Worst;
  2600	            SlackMargin = p->pSuperLib ? p->pSuperLib->tDelayInv.Worst : 0.0;
  2601	            if ( Slack <= SlackMargin + p->fEpsilon )
  2602	                return 0;
  2603	            return Map_MatchCutHasStmapFanoutRisk( pNode, pCut );
  2604	        }
  2605	        return 0;
  2606	    }
  2607	    if ( p->fSkipFanout >= 8 && p->fSkipFanout <= 57 )
  2608	    {
  2609	        if ( p->fMappingMode == 0 )
  2610	        {
  2611	            if ( p->vMapObjs->nSize <= 20000 )
  2612	                return 0;
  2613	            return Map_MatchCutHasStmapFanoutRisk( pNode, pCut );
  2614	        }
  2615	        if ( p->fMappingMode >= 1 && p->fMappingMode <= 3 && p->vMapObjs->nSize <= 8000 )
  2616	        {
  2617	            pCutBest = pNode->pCutBest[fPhase];
  2618	            if ( pCutBest == NULL )
  2619	                return 0;
  2620	            pMatchBest = pCutBest->M + fPhase;
  2621	            if ( pMatchBest->pSuperBest == NULL )
  2622	                return 0;
  2623	            Slack = pNode->tRequired[fPhase].Worst - pMatchBest->tArrive.Worst;
  2624	            SlackMargin = p->pSuperLib ? p->pSuperLib->tDelayInv.Worst : 0.0;
  2625	            if ( Slack <= SlackMargin + p->fEpsilon )
  2626	                return 0;
  2627	            return Map_MatchCutHasStmapFanoutRisk( pNode, pCut );
  2628	        }
  2629	        if ( p->fMappingMode == 1 )
  2630	        {
  2631	            pCutBest = pNode->pCutBest[fPhase];
  2632	            if ( pCutBest == NULL )
  2633	                return 0;
  2634	            pMatchBest = pCutBest->M + fPhase;
  2635	            if ( pMatchBest->pSuperBest == NULL )
  2636	                return 0;
  2637	            Slack = pNode->tRequired[fPhase].Worst - pMatchBest->tArrive.Worst;
  2638	            SlackMargin = p->pSuperLib ? p->pSuperLib->tDelayInv.Worst : 0.0;
  2639	            if ( Slack <= SlackMargin + p->fEpsilon )
  2640	                return 0;
  2641	            return Map_MatchCutHasStmapFanoutRisk( pNode, pCut );
  2642	        }
  2643	        return 0;
  2644	    }
  2645	    return (pNode->nRefs > 3 && pCut->nLeaves > 2) || (pNode->nRefs > 1 && pCut->nLeaves > 3);
  2646	}
  2647	
  2648	/**Function*************************************************************
  2649	
  2650	  Synopsis    [Returns 1 if an stmap area-sensitive mode should reject a cut.]
  2651	
  2652	  Description [The area-sensitive guards are evaluated after matching the
  2653	  candidate cut because their hypotheses need the candidate's actual mapper
  2654	  area. In stmap6 exact-area recovery, a wide high-fanout cut is rejected only
  2655	  when the existing match has slack and the candidate does not save at least
  2656	  half an inverter area. In stmap7, the area-saving override additionally
  2657	  requires more than two inverter delays of slack and excludes the highest
  2658	  fanout-risk bucket. In stmap8, the override is further rejected when the
  2659	  candidate match worsens mapper arrival by more than one quarter of an
  2660	  inverter delay. In stmap9, middle-slack candidates can recover area only
  2661	  when they are outside the highest fanout bucket, do not worsen mapper
  2662	  arrival, and save at least one inverter area. In stmap10, that middle-slack
  2663	  relief also requires the mapper node to be no deeper than level 96. In
  2664	  stmap11, that depth window is tightened to level 64. In stmap12, the same
  2665	  tight-depth relief is allowed only in the lower moderate fanout bucket. In
  2666	  stmap13, the same decision path also records exact-area guard statistics. In
  2667	  stmap14, the lower-moderate middle-slack relief must save two inverter areas
  2668	  instead of one. In stmap15, one-inverter relief is restored only after the
  2669	  mapper observes a high lower-moderate/middle-slack profile. In stmap16, the
  2670	  same behavior also reports each admitted relief choice. In stmap17, the
  2671	  adaptive one-inverter relief threshold also requires a material mapper-arrival
  2672	  improvement; arrival-neutral choices keep the stricter two-inverter fallback.
  2673	  In stmap18, the same decisions are preserved while near-miss rejected
  2674	  middle-slack candidates are logged. In stmap19, pre-profile one-inverter
  2675	  relief is seeded only by candidates with the same material arrival gain. In
  2676	  stmap20, the pre-profile seed is further limited to shallow nodes. In
  2677	  stmap21, deeper pre-profile seeds are allowed only with a much larger mapper
  2678	  arrival gain. In stmap22, the same deep-seed path is relaxed to a moderate
  2679	  mapper-arrival gain. In stmap23, moderate deep seeds also need near-critical
  2680	  existing mapper slack. In stmap24, the moderate deep slack gate is tightened
  2681	  to the lower edge of the critical slack window. In stmap25, the stmap24 rule
  2682	  is kept, and one moderate deep seed from the next slack band is admitted for
  2683	  ablation. In stmap26, the first such moderate seed is skipped and only the
  2684	  second qualifying seed is admitted. In stmap27, moderate deep candidates in
  2685	  the next slack band are admitted unless they match the high-reference,
  2686	  low-slack, stronger-moderate signature isolated by stmap26. In stmap28, that
  2687	  hard blocker is replaced by a soft area-margin penalty on high-reference
  2688	  moderate seeds whose slack and arrival-gain ratios resemble load-risky
  2689	  choices. In stmap29, the high-reference penalty is made continuous over the
  2690	  same seed band. In stmap30, the same load-risk surface is extended to
  2691	  high-reference strong deep seeds with a smaller continuous penalty. In
  2692	  stmap31, the strong-seed gain slope is damped to probe the penalty threshold.
  2693	  In stmap32, a cut-leaf load proxy adds a candidate-cut fanout-shape term to
  2694	  the strong-seed penalty. In stmap33, the cut-leaf term is normalized by the
  2695	  selected supergate fanout limit to test a library-derived drive/load signal.
  2696	  In stmap34, the drive-normalized term uses a bounded sublinear curve so high
  2697	  load/drive ratios do not automatically saturate the area margin. Stmap35
  2698	  preserves the stmap34 mapper decision surface and adds direct SCL
  2699	  mapped-load diagnostics in the command layer. Stmap36 feeds the aggregate
  2700	  direct SCL load/max-cap severity from a first pass back into the same bounded
  2701	  strong-seed area margin. Stmap37 restricts that SCL feedback to first-pass
  2702	  over-cap hotspot AIG IDs and their immediate mapped fanin cone. Stmap38
  2703	  broadens the first-pass association and checks candidate cut leaves against
  2704	  that hotspot set before applying the local SCL feedback term. Stmap39 carries
  2705	  first-pass SCL load pressure onto downstream consumers and their fanin cones,
  2706	  then lets strong-seed candidates use the maximum node/cut consumer pressure
  2707	  as the local SCL feedback term. Stmap40 keeps the same pressure source but
  2708	  passes it as a dense AIG-ID vector so medium-pressure reconstructed IDs survive
  2709	  the command-to-mapper boundary. Stmap41 keeps dense pressure but gates its
  2710	  strength by first-pass SCL criticality, reducing non-critical over-cap hits
  2711	  before they affect mapper area recovery. Stmap42 instead propagates the
  2712	  over-cap consumer's criticality into the fanin cone and keeps a bounded
  2713	  raw-pressure floor for sink-correlated load pressure. Stmap43 keeps the
  2714	  strong-seed sink-pressure rule but applies moderate deep penalties only when
  2715	  the candidate or its leaves have material sink-pressure, letting unpressured
  2716	  area-recovery candidates use the lighter one-inverter margin. Stmap44 keeps
  2717	  the same strong-seed pressure protection and tightens moderate recovery by
  2718	  requiring direct node pressure and cut-leaf pressure to agree. Stmap45 moves
  2719	  that agreement check into the moderate-admission rule, so disagreement keeps
